A bit more than 10 Euro:

> With effect from 1 July 2003 at the latest, charges levied by an
> institution in respect of cross-border credit transers in euro up to
> EUR 12 500 shall be the same as the charges levied by the same
> institution in respect of corresponding credit transfers in euro
> transacted within the Member State in which the establishment of that
> institution executing the cross-border transfer is located.
> 
> With effect from 1 January 2006 the amount EUR 12 500 shall be raised
> to EUR 50 000.

The same rules are also applied for transfers between GBP and EUR. In 
effect this means that there are no bank charges for receiving euro 
payments into my sterling account. The only difference is that when I 
receive euros the bank writes me a letter to tell me, but they don't charge 
me for it.


